Work in the Cantor space $2^\omega$ equipped with the standard product (fair-coin) measure $\mu$. For a tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$, let $[T]\subseteq 2^\omega$ denote the set of infinite branches of $T$. Recall the following three principles (in the language of second-order arithmetic, e.g. over $\mathsf{RCA}_0$):

- $P^+$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there is a subtree $S\subseteq T$ that is perfect (every node of $S$ has two incomparable extensions in $S$) and satisfies $\mu([S])>0$;
- $P$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there is a perfect subtree $S\subseteq T$;
- $P^-$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there exists a sequence $(X_n:n\in\omega)$ of pairwise distinct elements with $X_n\in[T]$ for all $n$.

Now replace the notion "$\mu([T])>0$" by a notion based on dimension. Concretely, for a fixed real number $s\in(0,1]$ (or, alternatively, using effective Hausdorff dimension), declare a tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ to be *dimension-positive* if the Hausdorff dimension of $[T]$ is at least $s$ (respectively, positive, or equal to $1$). Define the dimension analogues $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, $P^-_{\dim}$ by repeating the three statements above with "$\mu([T])>0$" replaced by "$T$ is dimension-positive" (and similarly "$\mu([S])>0$" replaced by "$S$ is dimension-positive" in $P^+_{\dim}$).

Problem. Give precise formulations of $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, and $P^-_{\dim}$. Determine, over $\mathsf{RCA}_0$ (or in the appropriate computability-theoretic / reverse-mathematics framework), which implications and non-implications hold among $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, and $P^-_{\dim}$, and compare them with the corresponding implication/non-implication results known in the measure-based setting for $P^+$, $P$, and $P^-$.

